<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Two public pages, and deliberately only two.

  NOT LISTED, both on purpose: admin.html, which is noindex and linked from
  nowhere, so listing it would advertise the one path this site never publishes
  (see public/robots.txt for the same reasoning at more length); and 404.html,
  which is an error page and never belongs in a sitemap.

  NO <lastmod>, also on purpose. The element is optional, and a hardcoded date
  in a static file goes stale the first time somebody edits a page and forgets
  this one. A lastmod that lies is worse than none at all, because a search
  engine that catches it stops trusting the signal for the whole site. <loc>
  alone is a valid sitemap and it can never rot. If dated entries are ever
  wanted, generate them from git at build time rather than typing them here.

  Note this file DOES work today, unlike robots.txt beside it: a sitemap is
  valid for any URL at or below its own path, so /bssaub-website/sitemap.xml
  covers both pages below. What it loses at this path is discovery through
  robots.txt, so until the custom domain lands it has to be submitted by hand
  in Search Console. The URLs are absolute because the format requires it, and
  they flip to the custom domain with the canonical tags in the HTML.
-->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://chaos-961.github.io/bssaub-website/</loc>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://chaos-961.github.io/bssaub-website/account</loc>
  </url>
</urlset>
